===Etymology 1===
The {{glossary|noun}} is an {{initialism|en|deoxyribonucleic acid|nocap=1}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=56224|date=September 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|genetics}}{{lb|en|biochemistry|genetics}} {{initialism of|en|deoxyribonucleic acid|t=a [[nucleic acid]] [[find#Verb|found]] in all [[living thing]]s (and some [[non-living]] things such as certain [[virus#Noun|viruses]]) which [[consist#Verb|consists]] of [[two]] [[polynucleotide]] [[chain#Noun|chains]] that [[coil#Verb|coil]] around each other to [[form#Verb|form]] a [[double#Adjective|double]] [[helix#Noun|helix]]; [[encode#Verb|encoded]] in its [[structure#Noun|structure]] are [[genetic]] [[instruction]]s for [[development]], [[functioning#Noun|functioning]], [[growth]], and [[reproduction]]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Martin Kamen|Martin D[avid] Kamen]]|title=Detection of Intermediates, Criteria of Purity|editor=Louis H. Roddis|journal=Supplement to the United States Naval Medical Bulletin on Preparation and Measurement of Isotopes and Some of Their Medical Aspects|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Bureau of Medicine and Surgery}}, [[w:United States Navy|U.S. Navy]]; [[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|month=March–April|year=1948|page=118|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Bp0aAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA118|oclc=11256905|passage=Among the various fractions isolated was one presumed to contain only desoxyribosenucleic acid ('''DNA'''). Because of certain speculations on the role of nucleic acids in protein synthesis it was necessary to measure and compare the rate of phosphate entry into '''DNA''' with that in other fractions of yeast phosphate.|footer={{small|Desoxyribosenucleic acid is an archaic name of deoxyribonucleic acid.}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:James Watson|J[ames] D[ewey] Watson]]; [[w:Francis Crick|F[rancis] H[arry] C[ompton] Crick]]|title=Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids: A Structure for Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid|journal=[[w:Nature (journal)|Nature: A Weekly Illustrated Journal of Science]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150403234208/http://www.nature.com/nature/dna50/watsoncrick.pdf|location=London|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan and Co.]]; New York, N.Y.: [[w:Macmillan Inc.|The Macmillan Company]]|date=25 April 1953|volume=171|issue=4356|page=737|column=1|oclc=64051812|passage=We wish to suggest a structure for the salt of deoxyribose nucleic acid ('''D.N.A.'''). This structure has novel features which are of considerable biological interest.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Arthur Kornberg}} (witness)|chapter=Statement of Dr. Arthur Kornberg, Professor and Executive Head of the Department of Biochemistry, Stanford University School of Medicine, Palo Alto, Calif.|title=National Commission on Health Science and Society: Hearings before the Subcommittee on Government Research of the [[w:United States Senate Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs|Committee on Government Operations]], {{w|United States Senate}}, Ninetieth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session on S.J. Res. 145: A Joint Resolution for the Establishment of the National Commission on Health Science and Society{{nb...|March 7, 8, 21, 22, 27, and 28; April 2, 1968: Printed for the Use of the Committee on Government Operations}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=8 March 1968|page=40|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nao3AAAAIAAJ&pg=RA2-PA40|oclc=453502|passage=As you know, heredity resides in our genes. Our genes are, in turn, composed of complex molecules called '''DNA'''. About 10 years ago we learned how to synthesize '''DNA''' in the test tube with the use of a certain cellular catalyst or enzyme. {{...}} During the past year, we have been able to synthesize '''DNA''' which has the full genetic activity of natural '''DNA'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John W. Hicks|title=DNA Profiling: A Tool for Law Enforcement|editor=Thomas J. Deakin|journal=w:FBI Law Enforcement Bulletin|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Federal Bureau of Investigation}}, {{w|United States Department of Justice}}; Office of Congressional and Public Affairs|month=August|year=1988|volume=57|issue=8|page=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lPZeYx7YDMkC&pg=RA7-PA1|column=3|issn=0014-5688|oclc=1319799735|passage=Recent breakthroughs in '''DNA''' technology are expected to provide investigators with powerful forensic tools to help solve these difficult kinds of cases.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Tina Henkin|Tina M. Henkin]]; Joseph E. Peters|chapter=Bacterial Genetic Analysis: Fundamentals and Current Approaches|title=Snyder & Champness: Molecular Genetics of Bacteria|edition=5th|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:American Society for Microbiology|ASM Press]]|year=2020|page=157|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6-TxDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA157|column=1|isbn=978-1-55581-975-0|passage=[I]n bacteria, recombination between the '''DNAs''' of different organisms usually occurs between a piece of '''DNA''' from one strain of a bacterium, called the donor strain, and the entire chromosome of another strain, called the recipient strain.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|loosely}} The [[part#Noun|part]] of a living thing that [[carry#Verb|carries]] genetic [[information]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The [[fundamental#Adjective|fundamental]] [[nature#Noun|nature]] or [[value#Noun|values]] of a [[person#Noun|person]], or an [[organization]] or other thing, especially when [[consider]]ed as [[innate#Adjective|innate]] and/or [[immutable#Adjective|immutable]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kevin [John] Kennedy; Mary Moore|chapter=The Predictable Challenges Faced by Dominant Companies|title=Going the Distance: Why Some Companies Dominate and Others Fail|location=Upper Saddle River, N.J.|publisher=[[w:FT Press|Financial Times Prentice Hall]]|year=2003|page=14|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tgdyUfysdJ8C&pg=PA14|isbn=978-0-13-046120-9|passage=These ingredients in a  company's '''DNA''' mean that [the] company will attract and grow leaders with these qualities.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Bill McBean|chapter=Fact 1: If You Don’t Lead, No One Will Follow|title=The Facts of Business Life: What Every Successful Business Owner Knows that You Don’t|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|John Wiley & Sons]]|year=2012|page=58|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yqgsEv5fnQwC&pg=PA58|isbn=978-1-118-09496-9|passage=The main leadership priority at Level 2 is creating the business's '''DNA''' by defining how all the moving parts of the company will work, both independently and  together.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ryan Bort|title=The Real Reason Republicans are Loading Their 2022 Campaign Ads with Guns|magazine=w:Rolling Stone|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209033252/https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/guns-republican-primary-campaign-ads-midterms-1336903/|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Penske Media Corporation|date=13 April 2022|issn=0035-791X|oclc=1057982905|passage=But this new fixation on guns is {{...}} coming from the hardcore MAGA set, and not only is it likely to stick around beyond the primaries, it's likely hardwired into the '''DNA''' of a party now driven by extremism, conspiracy, and a belief that violence is a legitimate tool to achieve desired political outcomes.}}

